## Do Notation

https://mightybyte.github.io/monad-challenges/pages/ex5-1.html

A `do` block with `MCPrelude` will look like:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
import MCPrelude

def rule1 do
  m do
    foo <- calcFoo()
    bar(foo)
  end
end
```

It will be expanded to:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
def rule1 do
  calcFoo()
  |> Monad.bind(fn foo -> bar(foo) end)
end
```

```elixir
# Copy your data declarations and monad protocol and implementations
```

## Do Notation – operators

https://mightybyte.github.io/monad-challenges/pages/ex5-2.html

Ignore the need for `fail`, we defined the protocol ourselves.

Don't redefine the `Monad` protocol.  
You could define an operator `~>>` like so though:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
def m ~>> f, do: Monad.bind(m, f)
```

But it's not that useful due to Elixir's use of `m |> Monad.bind(f)`

